The need for understanding the fundamental photophysical processes in organo metal halide perovskite (OMHP) solar cells has become more evident in view of the swift rise in efficiency of these devices.1–6 However, literature provides limited details of the charge carrier dynamics, i.e. from charge generation to transport and finally their extraction to the electrodes. We and others have reported injection of electrons from OMHPs to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ just only very recently.6–8 Other timeresolved studies have started to reveal several other unique properties of this material, e.g. high mobilities of electrons and holes,9 slow recombination of charges2,6,10 and diffusion lengths of micrometers.6,11 Although theoretical calculations indicate balanced electron and hole mobilities,12–14 different groups report widely varying values for electron and hole mobilities in the perovskite.7,15 Additionally, it is unclear to what extent OMHPs can be regarded as genuine intrinsic semiconductors. Recent research has pointed out that under specific preparation conditions OMHPs may behave as ntype 16–18 or p-type semiconductor.18–21 It is therefore necessary to determine the role of dark carriers in understanding its photophysical properties.
In this study, we report on the electron and hole injection and recombination dynamics of two frequently used chargespecific organic electrodes: the electron acceptor [6,6]-phenylC61-butyric acid methyl ester, PCBM, and the hole acceptor Spiro-OMeTAD.8,11,22–24 Thin polycrystalline films of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ were prepared by spin-coating $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in a stoichiometric ratio from a $\gamma$ -butyrolactone solution in a nitrogenfilled glovebox. Results of XRD measurements showing tetragonal structure of the perovskite can be found in Fig. S1. PCBM or Spiro-OMeTAD was spin-coated the on top of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . All samples have not been exposed to ambient conditions at any time before and during the electrical measurements. By time-resolved, electrodeless conductivity measurements, using terahertz (THz) or microwave (MW) radiation as probe, we are able to monitor the photo-induced charge carrier formation and decay from sub-ps timescales up to one microsecond. We note that the instrument response function (IRF) of our THz set-up is about 0.2 ps while for MW it is $18~\mathrm{ns}$ . Moreover, due to the superior signal to noise of the MW setup, the excitation densities that can be used are at least an order of magnitude lower than for time-resolved THz spectroscopy (TRTS). Other details of the growth method, crosssectional SEM images of the samples as well as the description of the time resolved techniques could be found in the supporting information (SI).
We found that on optical excitation of the perovskite, charge transfer from $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ into Spiro-OMeTAD occurs on the subps time scale, but is much slower, from few hundreds of ps to several ns, with PCBM as electron accepting electrode. The difference in the injection rate was assigned to differences in energy band alignment at the interface. Furthermore, by using the photo-induced time-resolved microwave conductivity (TRMC) technique, the charge carrier recombination back to the ground state was found to be at least one order of magnitude faster in the $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}/P C B M}$ (few ns to few tens of ns) than in the MAPbI3/Spiro-OMeTAD (hundreds of ns to several ${\upmu\mathrm{s}}_{,}$ ). We surmise that this faster decay in MAPbI3/PCBM is due to interfacial recombination between electrons injected into PCBM with both photoinduced and dark holes in MAP${\mathfrak{b l}}_{3}$ . For the MAPbI3/Spiro-OMeTAD, the photo-excited electrons undergo a similar decay route as in the neat $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ . This suggests that the total concentration of holes in $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ does not appreciably change upon injection of photo-generated holes into Spiro-OMeTAD. Altogether, our results suggest that the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ studied here is an unintentionally p-type doped semiconductor, which controls the charge carrier dynamics into organic electrodes.
Plotted in Figure 1(a) are the TRTS kinetics of neat $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , MAPbI3/PCBM and MAPbI3/Spiro-OMeTAD. The samples are excited at $590~\mathrm{nm}$ through the organic layer, which at this wavelength does not substantially absorb the incident laser light. Although the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ layer consists of large, micrometer-sized granular structures according to surface profilometry, the light penetration depth at this wavelength into $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ is only about $200\ \mathrm{nm}.$ .26  The TRTS signal size is expressed as the product of the quantum yield, $\varphi,$ and the sum of electron and hole mobility $(\mu_{e}+\mu_{h})$ . This product is calculated according to:
$$
\varphi\Big(\mu_{e}+\mu_{h}\Big)=\frac{\Delta\sigma L}{e I_{\phantom{e}_{0}}F_{\phantom{e}_{A}}}
$$
where $\Delta\sigma$ is the measured change in conductivity, $L$ the thickness, $e$ is the elementary charge, $I_{0}$ is the number of photons per unit area per pulse and $F_{A}$ is the fraction of absorbed light. The value of $\varphi$ is assumed to be close to unity at short timescales. Analogous to our previous work,6,25 the maximum initial signal size for the neat $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ is ${\sim}15\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{Vs}$ due to $\mathcal{\mu}_{e}$ $+\mu_{h})$ . After 1 ns, the signal has slightly decayed due to second order recombination as reported previously. Interestingly, the samples with acceptor layers show a very different behavior. For MAPbI3/PCBM, the initial signal size is similar to that of the neat $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ but decays much faster. This rapid decay is clearly seen on a 7 ns time window, Fig. 1(b). For MAP$\left.\mathtt{b l}_{3}\right.^{}$ /Spiro-OMeTAD, the TRTS signal is about three times smaller than for neat $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , close to $5\mathrm{cm}^{2}/\mathrm{Vs}$ and approximately constant over the first ns.
